---
description: Never commit, push, or otherwise write to git history without being asked
alwaysApply: true
---

# Git is manual

The user commits and pushes themselves. Never run these on your own initiative,
even when a task feels finished and even when the changes are obviously correct:

- `git commit` / `git commit --amend`
- `git push`
- `git merge`, `git rebase`, `git cherry-pick`, `git revert`
- `git stash`, `git reset`, `git checkout -- <file>`, `git restore`
- `gh pr create`, `gh pr merge`

Only run them when the user asks in that turn, e.g. "commit this" or "push it".
A general instruction to build or fix something is not permission to commit.

Leave finished work as uncommitted changes in the working tree and say what you
changed, so the user can review and stage it.

Reading git state is always fine: `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`,
`git show`, `git branch`.
